  {
    "id": "vellore-mutiny-1806",
    "slug": "vellore-mutiny",
    "name": "Vellore Mutiny",
    "aliases": [
      "Vellore Rebellion",
      "Vellore Revolt"
    ],
    "summary": "On 10 July 1806 sepoys of the Madras army seized Vellore Fort, killed about 130 British troops, and raised the Mysore flag. Tipu Sultan’s sons, lodged in the fort since 1799, were present. The immediate grievance was new dress regulations — headgear, shaving, ornaments, caste marks — read as an attack on Hindu and Muslim practice, with pay behind it. Gillespie’s relief from Arcot retook the fort the same day. Hundreds of sepoys were killed in the fighting or in executions, some blown from guns. Colonial conquest had already taken Mysore; Vellore was the garrison answering back.",
    "scale": "medium",
    "scaleRationale": "A one-day mutiny, but the first large violent rising of Company sepoys, and a rehearsal of 1857’s religious grievance.",
