  {
    "id": "first-afghan-war",
    "slug": "first-afghan-war",
    "name": "First Anglo-Afghan War",
    "aliases": [
      "First Afghan War",
      "Retreat from Kabul"
    ],
    "summary": "In 1839 the Company invaded Afghanistan to replace Dost Mohammad with Shah Shuja and block a supposed Russian advance. Kabul was occupied; the occupation was not accepted. In January 1842 some 4,500 British and Indian troops and about 12,000 camp followers marched out of Kabul under a promise of safe conduct. The column was destroyed in the passes. An Army of Retribution re-entered Kabul that autumn, burned the bazaar, and left. Dost Mohammad returned. Colonial conquest failed as occupation and was remembered as a massacre on the road.",
    "scale": "large",
    "scaleRationale": "A three-year invasion whose destruction of the Kabul column was among the worst British military disasters of the century.",
